WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.900
The H P R version two seventy-two-kilowatt shelf provides a current component

2
00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:04.360
example.

3
00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:08.900
Its cited options use six twelve-kilowatt power supply unit slots and state

4
00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:11.740
sixty kilowatts with N plus one and dual cords.

5
00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:15.680
The document also defines the input arrangement and a fifty volt output.

6
00:00:16.219 --> 00:00:17.420
Keep those statements together.

7
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:21.880
They do not mean every high-power rack delivers sixty kilowatts, or that

8
00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:24.320
every dual-cord failure leaves the same capacity.

9
00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:28.000
Review the exact option, connection and operating conditions.

10
00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:32.720
N plus one describes spare unit capacity relative to a requirement; it

11
00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:37.060
doesn't automatically provide independent redundancy across every shared part of the system.

12
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:42.280
Component specifications for the power monitoring module and connectors support this integration.

13
00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:45.700
They help establish the relevant interfaces, but we won't turn this rack

14
00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:47.720
course into a register or pinout survey.

15
00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:51.960
The practical task is to verify that the configured shelf, distribution and

16
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.820
equipment combination supports the required workload and fault behavior using consistent document

17
00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:57.360
revisions.

